# Example 01:

# Let's start by getting the names of the protein Q9NPB9:

match
$p isa protein, has id "Q9NPB9", has name $n;
fetch { "name": $n };

# We should see nine name attributes. If we look closely at the schema, we can see that these attributes actually have
# three types: primary-uniprot-name, alternative-uniprot-name, and uniprot-entry-name. All three are subtypes of name,
# so they are valid results for the query. This is an example of TypeDB's polymorphism at work: a single query returns
# results of different types. The same applies to id, which matches the protein's uniprot-id here.

